Theorem IC-6: FipContract sum-type tier join soundness + post-realization timing

  Preconditions:
    - IC-1 has decomposed the CallGraph into SCCs in forward
      topological order (callees before callers) per
      Annex E §AIMS IC-1 + sec-7 PL-1a
    - IC-2 (proven separately) seeds ParamContract bottoms; IC-3
      (proven separately) joins ParamContract toward conservative;
      IC-4 (proven separately) extracts ReturnContract per
      function; IC-5 (proven separately) derives EffectSummary
      per function via per-instruction OR-fold + AND-fold + one-
      shot SCC assignment
    - IC-6 type signature (per Interprocedural.proof lines 42-47):
        join_fip_contract : List FipContract -> FipContract
      whose output is the per-function FIP classification, joined
      across all caller-call-site or branch-merge observations
      and emitted POST-REALIZATION (Step 5a per Annex E §AIMS) so the join consumes realized-IR proof evidence,
      not Step-1 lattice-only proxies
    - Spec statement (Annex E §AIMS IC-6): "FIP contract:
      `Never` absorbs all; `Conditional` absorbs `Bounded /
      Certified`; `Bounded(n) join Bounded(m) = Bounded(max(n,m))`.
      `FipContract::Certified IFF zero unmatched alloc / dealloc`
      in realized IR. **Timing**: POST-REALIZATION (Step 5a).
      Step 1 inits `Never`. PL-1a ensures callees realized before
      callers. FipContract does NOT re-enter Step 1 fixpoint."
    - The FipContract value space is a 4-tier sum type per the
      shipped enum at compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/
      contract/mod.rs lines 386-416:
        FipContract::Never -- "definitely not FIP"
        FipContract::Conditional -- "FIP with preconditions"
        FipContract::Bounded(allocation_budget: u32) -- "FBIP with bounded alloc"
        FipContract::Certified -- "Fully In-Place, zero alloc / dealloc"
      ordered along the precision chain
        Never < Conditional < Bounded(n) < Certified
      where the absorption rule produces the canonical join:
        Never join X = Never (Never absorbs all)
        Conditional join X = Conditional when X in {Bounded, Certified}
                             (Conditional absorbs Bounded / Certified)
        Bounded(n) join Bounded(m) = Bounded(max(n, m))
        Bounded(n) join Certified = Bounded(n)
                             (Certified contributes no extra alloc;
                              Bounded budget governs)
        Certified join Certified = Certified
    - Shipped engine: `FipContract::join` at 
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/contract/mod.rs lines 418-444
      implements the 4-tier absorption table verbatim; reused as
      the join operator by interprocedural extraction (Step 1
      seed) and by Step 5a verification (post-realization
      authoritative computation)
    - Realization-time proof engine: `verify_fip_contract` at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/verify/fip.rs lines
      75-200 walks the realized ArcFunction and counts unmatched
      alloc / dealloc per FIP claim; downgrade rules per IC-6
      spec
    - Timing contract — POST-REALIZATION (Step 5a):
      Annex E §AIMS Pipeline Ordering Step 5a
      `verify_fip_contract` runs AFTER Step 5
      (`realize_rc_reuse`) materializes RC / reuse / arg-
      ownership instructions and BEFORE Step 6 (structural
      verify) consumes them. The FipContract emitted by Step 1
      interprocedural extraction is PROVISIONAL — seeded `Never`
      per Annex E §AIMS IC-6 "Step 1 inits `Never`" — and
      receives its authoritative value only after Step 5a
      observes the realized IR's alloc / dealloc balance
    - PL-1a guarantees callees are realized (Step 5a applied)
      BEFORE callers enter Step 5a, so cross-function joins
      consume final FipContract values, not Step-1 provisional
      seeds
    - Per the Salsa deterministic query contract
      (compiler.md sec-Salsa "Queries must be pure"), the
      same {realized ArcFunction, callee MemoryContract set,
      target triple} input set produces the same FipContract
      output across compilation runs; the absorption table at
      lines 418-444 is a pure total function with no hash-map
      iteration order dependency
    - L-1 (commutativity of join), L-2 (associativity of join),
      L-4 (partial-order axiom), L-6 (monotonicity over the
      precision chain) lemmas from §AIMS §3 are proven in sec-02
      of this plan (Plan-Bug DAG) and provide the lattice-property
      foundation for the 4-tier sum-type join

  Soundness property:
    Forall function `f` with N >= 0 observed `FipContract` claims
    `[fc_1, fc_2, ..., fc_N]` arising from caller call-sites
    AND / OR per-branch contract emission within `f`.
      Let `c := join_fip_contract([fc_1, ..., fc_N])` be the
      emitted result. Six conjuncts SHALL hold per spec
      Annex E §AIMS IC-6 + sec-7 PL-1a + sec-9 VF-6:

      (P1) Never absorbs all:
        For all fc_i: fc_i = Never IMPLIES c = Never
        per the absorption rule "Never absorbs all" at sec-5
        IC-6 + contract/mod.rs line 421. Never is the lattice
        TOP of the FipContract precision chain — the "most
        conservative" claim. Any single Never observation
        forces the joined contract to Never, which is the
        soundness floor: a function with ANY non-FIP caller
        cannot be certified FIP.

      (P2) Conditional absorbs Bounded / Certified:
        For all fc_i in {Bounded(_), Certified}:
          fc_i join Conditional = Conditional
        per the absorption rule "Conditional absorbs Bounded /
        Certified" at sec-5 IC-6 + contract/mod.rs lines 425-
        430. Conditional is the second-most-conservative claim:
        FIP-eligible only when preconditions hold. Bounded and
        Certified are both "stronger" claims (zero or bounded
        alloc), but joining with Conditional widens to the
        less-precise Conditional because the precondition
        constraint dominates.

      (P3) Bounded budget max-join:
        Bounded(n) join Bounded(m) = Bounded(max(n, m))
        per the absorption rule "Bounded(n) join Bounded(m) =
        Bounded(max(n, m))" at sec-5 IC-6 + contract/mod.rs
        lines 432-437. The allocation budget grows monotonically
        across observations — the joined budget is the maximum
        of the per-observation budgets, never smaller (under-
        approximating budget would allow more allocation than
        the realized IR proves, violating VF-6 "contracts and
        realization must agree" per Annex E §AIMS).

      (P4) Bounded join Certified = Bounded:
        Bounded(n) join Certified = Bounded(n)
        per contract/mod.rs lines 432-437 (the `Bounded` arm
        join `Certified` falls through to the budget-preserving
        result). Certified contributes zero alloc + zero
        dealloc, so the Bounded(n) budget remains the binding
        constraint after the join. The result is NOT Certified
        because the joined function may allocate up to n;
        Certified would falsely claim zero alloc.

      (P5) Certified IFF zero unmatched alloc / dealloc:
        c = Certified IFF the realized ArcFunction has zero
        unmatched allocation instructions (`Construct`,
        `Reuse`, `CollectionReuse`, `PartialApply`) AND zero
        unmatched deallocation events (`RcDec` triggering free
        OR `Drop` finalization) at the post-realization
        observation point (Step 5a per sec-7 PL-1a). The
        bi-implication is the load-bearing soundness contract
        per Annex E §AIMS VF-6 ("contracts and realization
        must agree") + sec-7 PL-5 ("no pass relies on stale
        summaries") + Annex E §AIMS §2 invariant 1 ("contracts
        and realization must agree").

      (P6) Timing POST-REALIZATION (Step 5a):
        c is emitted at Step 5a of the AIMS pipeline (post-
        Step-5 `realize_rc_reuse`, pre-Step-6 structural
        verify) per Annex E §AIMS Pipeline Ordering. The
        Step 1 interprocedural extraction emits a provisional
        FipContract::Never seed; the authoritative value is
        computed AFTER realization observes the IR's alloc /
        dealloc balance. PL-1a ensures callees complete Step 5a
        before callers enter Step 5a, so cross-function
        absorption (P1, P2) consumes finalized FipContract
        values across SCC boundaries. FipContract does NOT
        re-enter the Step 1 fixpoint per sec-5 IC-6 + sec-5
        IC-7 ("FipContract excluded (post-realization)"
        explicitly removes FipContract from the IC-7
        convergence formula).

  Proof obligation:
    Six-part discharge corresponding to (P1)..(P6); absorption-
    table conjuncts (P1)..(P4) ground in the shipped Rust match
    at contract/mod.rs lines 418-444; certification soundness
    (P5) grounds in the `verify_fip_contract` realized-IR
    counting algorithm at fip.rs lines 75-200; timing soundness
    (P6) grounds in PL-1a's SCC topological ordering + Step 5a's
    placement after Step 5 in the pipeline (Annex E §AIMS
    Pipeline Ordering).

    Part (P1) — Never absorbs all:

      Shipped match arm (contract/mod.rs line 421):

        (FipContract::Never, _) | (_, FipContract::Never) =>
            FipContract::Never,

      The match arm fires symmetrically — `Never join X` AND
      `X join Never` both produce `Never` for any X in the
      4-tier FipContract space. Per L-1 (commutativity), the
      symmetric coverage is mandatory and verified by the
      `_` pattern in both positions.

      Soundness rationale (per sec-5 IC-6 + sec-9 VF-6):
      Never is the "definitely not FIP" claim — a single
      observation proving the function may allocate via a
      non-FIP path forces the joined contract to widen to
      Never. The absorption is the lattice TOP of the
      FipContract precision chain; folding Never with any
      stronger claim (Conditional / Bounded / Certified)
      yields Never because the disproof of FIP-eligibility
      cannot be revoked by adding a stronger claim from a
      different observation site.

      Per L-2 (associativity), the N-ary fold over a list
      containing any Never element collapses to Never
      regardless of fold order. (P1) discharged.

      Negative-direction witness: suppose hypothetical
      implementer makes Never absorbable by Certified
      (`(FipContract::Never, FipContract::Certified) =>
      FipContract::Certified`). Then a function with one
      Never-claim caller (proving a non-FIP path) and one
      Certified-claim caller (provisional, pre-realization
      seed) would emit Certified after the join. Step 5a's
      `verify_fip_contract` would then check realized IR
      against the (wrongly-certified) Certified claim, find
      the Never path's alloc / dealloc, and trigger the
      `FipStructural` wrap at pipeline/aims_pipeline/mod.rs
      line 269. The downgrade-on-realization path catches
      the bug, but only AFTER the wrong contract has been
      consumed by upstream callers in the SCC fixpoint —
      wasted convergence iterations + diagnostic noise. The
      absorption discipline at line 421 is therefore load-
      bearing for the Step-1 / Step-5a separation; weakening
      it shifts cost from Step 1 (correct) to Step 5a
      (recovery from wrong contracts).

    Part (P2) — Conditional absorbs Bounded / Certified:

      Shipped match arms (contract/mod.rs lines 425-430):

        (FipContract::Conditional, _) | (_, FipContract::Conditional) =>
            FipContract::Conditional,

      The Conditional arms fire symmetrically — `Conditional
      join X` AND `X join Conditional` both produce
      `Conditional` for any X in {Bounded(_), Certified}.
      Per L-1 + L-2, the symmetric coverage + associativity
      guarantee fold-order independence of the absorption.

      Note: the spec rule sec-5 IC-6 says "Conditional
      absorbs Bounded / Certified". The match at lines 425-
      430 covers ALL X (including Never, Conditional itself),
      but the Never case is already handled by line 421
      (Never absorbs all, including Conditional). The
      Conditional-self case yields Conditional trivially.
      The Bounded / Certified cases yield Conditional per the
      spec rule.

      Soundness rationale (per sec-5 IC-6):
      Conditional is the "FIP-eligible with preconditions"
      claim — meaningful only when paired with caller-side
      precondition verification. Bounded and Certified are
      "stronger" claims (specific alloc bounds) but they do
      NOT subsume Conditional's precondition constraint.
      Joining widens to Conditional because the precondition
      requirement dominates: the joined contract MUST
      preserve the precondition constraint to remain sound,
      even when one observation site claims a stronger
      property.

      Per L-6 (monotonicity over the precision chain),
      Conditional ≤ Bounded(_) ≤ Certified in the lattice
      partial order; joining a stronger claim WITH Conditional
      yields the weaker (Conditional) per the join-yields-LUB
      semantics. (P2) discharged.

    Part (P3) — Bounded budget max-join:

      Shipped match arm (contract/mod.rs lines 432-437):

        (FipContract::Bounded(n), FipContract::Bounded(m)) =>
            FipContract::Bounded(n.max(m)),

      The max-join is the standard finite-chain join over
      u32 budgets: per L-1 + L-2, max is commutative and
      associative on u32. Per L-6 (monotonicity), the budget
      can only grow when joined with a larger observation,
      never shrink — the converged budget is the LUB of all
      per-observation budgets.

      Soundness rationale (per sec-5 IC-6 + sec-9 VF-6):
      Under-approximating budget (min-join) would allow the
      realized IR to allocate more than the contract claims,
      triggering `verify_fip_contract` failure at Step 5a.
      Max-join is the soundness floor — the joined budget
      MUST accommodate every observation's claim. The
      `u32::max` operation is the canonical lattice join for
      finite-chain budget tracking (Birkhoff 1967 §I.5
      totally-ordered chains under max).

      Per L-6 (monotonicity), `max(n, m) >= n` and
      `max(n, m) >= m`, so the joined budget covers every
      per-observation budget. (P3) discharged.

      Negative-direction witness: suppose hypothetical
      implementer uses `n.min(m)` instead of `n.max(m)`:
        (FipContract::Bounded(n), FipContract::Bounded(m)) =>
            FipContract::Bounded(n.min(m)),

      Then a function with two observations (`Bounded(5)`
      and `Bounded(10)`) would emit `Bounded(5)` after the
      join. Step 5a's `verify_fip_contract` would check
      realized IR allocating 7 allocations on a path observed
      with `Bounded(10)`, and reject the contract because
      7 > 5. The realization-time downgrade catches the bug,
      but the contract was wrong from the start. Max-join
      is therefore load-bearing for the FBIP discipline;
      min-join is a soundness-floor violation.

    Part (P4) — Bounded join Certified = Bounded:

      Shipped match arms (contract/mod.rs lines 432-437,
      falling through after the Bounded-Bounded arm):

        (FipContract::Bounded(n), FipContract::Certified) |
        (FipContract::Certified, FipContract::Bounded(n)) =>
            FipContract::Bounded(n),

      The Bounded arm wins over Certified because Bounded(n)
      claims "at most n allocations" while Certified claims
      "zero allocations". When joined, the weaker claim
      (Bounded(n)) must prevail to cover both observation
      sites: the Bounded site may allocate up to n; the
      Certified site may not allocate. The joined contract
      Bounded(n) accommodates both — Certified would
      falsely claim zero alloc, allowing the Bounded site's
      n allocations to violate the contract.

      Per L-6 (monotonicity), Bounded(n) ≤ Certified in the
      precision chain, but for FipContract the "stronger"
      claim is Certified (zero alloc) and the "weaker" claim
      is Bounded(n) (at most n alloc). Join yields the LUB
      in the precision chain — the weaker claim Bounded(n).
      This is the correct lattice-join direction for a
      "soundness over precision" reading: under-approximation
      of allocation is unsound; over-approximation is sound
      but imprecise. (P4) discharged.

    Part (P5) — Certified IFF zero unmatched alloc / dealloc:

      Shipped match arm (contract/mod.rs line 443):

        (FipContract::Certified, FipContract::Certified) =>
            FipContract::Certified,

      The Certified-Certified arm is the trivial case:
      joining two functions both proven Certified yields
      Certified (zero alloc + zero dealloc still holds for
      the joined claim). The non-trivial soundness content
      is the IFF biconditional with the realized IR's
      alloc / dealloc balance.

      Realization-time verification (fip.rs lines 75-200):
      `verify_fip_contract` walks the realized ArcFunction
      and counts:
        - Allocation instructions: `Construct`, `Reuse`,
          `CollectionReuse`, `PartialApply` (per
          Annex E §AIMS IC-5 alloc-trigger table).
        - Deallocation events: `RcDec` triggering free
          (post-Step-5 RC realization), `Drop` finalization
          on owned values transitioning to dead.

      For `FipContract::Certified` to remain Certified after
      Step 5a, BOTH counts MUST be zero. ANY unmatched
      alloc OR ANY unmatched dealloc downgrades the contract:
        - Unmatched alloc only → Bounded(observed_count).
        - Unmatched dealloc only → Conditional (precondition:
          caller provides uniquely-owned input that can be
          consumed).
        - Both unmatched → Never.

      The IFF biconditional is enforced by the verification
      pass at fip.rs lines 75-200; failures produce
      `FipVerificationError` values wrapped as
      `VerifyError::FipStructural` per pipeline/aims_pipeline/
      mod.rs line 269 (first-pass wrap) + batch.rs line 231
      (second-pass batch wrap).

      Per Annex E §AIMS VF-6 ("contracts and
      realization must agree") + Annex E §AIMS §2 invariant
      1, the IFF is non-negotiable: a `Certified` contract
      with realized IR carrying unmatched alloc / dealloc is
      a soundness regression. The verification pass at
      Step 5a is the enforcement layer; the absorption table
      at contract/mod.rs lines 418-444 is the join operator
      consuming verified Certified values across SCC
      boundaries. (P5) discharged.

    Part (P6) — Timing POST-REALIZATION (Step 5a):

      Pipeline ordering (Annex E §AIMS Pipeline Ordering
      Steps 5 + 5a + 6):

        Step 5: realize_rc_reuse() — Phase 1: RC + reuse
                                            + arg_ownership
                                            (pre-merge)
        Step 5a: verify_fip_contract() — FIP enforcement
                                            verification
        Step 6: verify() — ARC IR structural
                                            sanity

      Step 5a observes the realized IR produced by Step 5 +
      computes the authoritative FipContract value per (P5).
      The Step 1 interprocedural extraction (Step 1 sec-7
      Pipeline Ordering) emits a provisional FipContract::Never
      seed per sec-5 IC-6 ("Step 1 inits `Never`"); reuse
      emission consumes this provisional value during Step 5,
      and Step 5a finalizes it AFTER Step 5 materializes the
      RC / reuse / arg-ownership instructions.

      Cross-function ordering (PL-1a):
      Per Annex E §AIMS PL-1a, the per-function pipeline
      (Steps 3-12) processes functions in SCC topological
      order, callees before callers. When function `f` enters
      Step 5a, every callee `g` referenced by `f` has already
      completed Step 5a, so `f`'s `verify_fip_contract` pass
      consumes finalized FipContract values from `g`'s
      MemoryContract.

      Cross-function absorption (P1, P2, P3, P4):
      When `f` joins FipContract claims across its callees'
      observation sites, the join operator at lines 418-444
      consumes the finalized (post-Step-5a) FipContract values
      per PL-1a's topological ordering. The Step-1 provisional
      Never seeds are overwritten by Step 5a's authoritative
      values BEFORE any caller observes them.

      Exclusion from IC-7 convergence:
      Per Annex E §AIMS IC-7 ("FipContract excluded
      (post-realization)"), the FipContract is explicitly
      removed from the IC-7 iteration bound formula
      (`param_count × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4` — 13 ParamContract +
      8 ReturnContract + 6 EffectSummary + 4 ContextBehavior;
      FipContract NOT a term). This is consistent with PL-1a's
      SCC topological ordering: FipContract does not re-enter
      the Step 1 fixpoint because its authoritative value is
      computed at Step 5a, AFTER the Step 1 fixpoint has
      converged on the other contract fields.

      Per Annex E §AIMS §2 invariant 3 ("no pass relies on
      stale summaries") + sec-7 PL-5 ("no pass shall rely on
      stale summaries"), the Step-1 provisional FipContract
      seed MUST be overwritten by Step 5a before any
      downstream consumer (RL-29 `noalias` emission, RL-30
      memory-attribute emission, FBIP enforcement) reads it.
      (P6) discharged.

      Negative-direction witness: suppose hypothetical
      implementer moves FipContract computation INTO the
      Step 1 interprocedural fixpoint (treating it as a
      lattice dimension like ParamContract / ReturnContract /
      EffectSummary). Then the IC-7 convergence formula would
      need a FipContract height term (currently absent per
      sec-5 IC-7); the SCC iteration would consume Step-1
      lattice-proxy FipContract values; and Step 5a's
      realized-IR verification would have to RE-ENTER Step 1
      to invalidate any stale FipContract::Certified claims.
      The pipeline ordering invariant PL-5 ("no stale
      summaries") would be violated — Step 5a's output would
      feed back into Step 1 inputs, creating a circular
      dependency. The Step-1 / Step-5a separation is therefore
      load-bearing for the AIMS pipeline acyclicity; merging
      them is a sequencing violation.

    Coherence side-conditions for (P1)..(P6):

      - Absorption-table coverage (P1)..(P4): the four arms
        plus the `Certified, Certified` arm cover every pair
        in the 4-tier FipContract value space (4 × 4 = 16
        pairs; the Never arms cover 7 pairs, the Conditional
        arms cover 5 pairs, the Bounded-Bounded arm covers
        1 pair, the Bounded-Certified arms cover 2 pairs,
        the Certified-Certified arm covers 1 pair; sum = 16).
        Exhaustiveness verified by Rust's match-exhaustiveness
        checker at contract/mod.rs lines 418-444.

      - Realization-time downgrade chain (P5): the IFF at
        (P5) is enforced by `verify_fip_contract` at fip.rs
        lines 75-200; the downgrade chain
        Certified → Bounded(n) | Conditional | Never reflects
        the realized IR's alloc / dealloc balance. The
        downgrade is monotone per L-6 (always widens, never
        narrows), so the verification pass cannot accidentally
        upgrade a Bounded / Conditional / Never contract to
        Certified.

      - Pipeline ordering invariant (P6): the Step-1 → Step-5a
        directional dependency is acyclic per sec-7 PL-1a;
        FipContract values flow only forward (Step 1
        provisional → Step 5 emission → Step 5a authoritative
        → downstream consumers). Backward flow would violate
        PL-5 ("no stale summaries") + AIMS Invariant 3.

      - L-1 + L-2 (commutativity + associativity) lemmas apply
        pointwise to (P1)..(P4): each absorption rule is
        symmetric (Never arms, Conditional arms, Bounded-
        Certified arms cover both join orderings); the max
        operation in (P3) is commutative + associative on u32.

      - L-6 (monotonicity) applies pointwise: every absorption
        rule maps to a result no smaller (in the precision
        chain) than the operands, so the N-ary fold over a
        list of FipContract claims yields a result that
        covers every per-observation claim.

    Convergence side-condition (cross-cite to IC-7):

      IC-7 (proven separately) bounds the SCC fixpoint
      iteration at `param_count × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4`, where
      FipContract is EXCLUDED from the iteration bound
      (sec-5 IC-7 + sec-5 IC-6 timing rule). The FipContract
      value is computed once per function at Step 5a, AFTER
      the Step 1 fixpoint has converged on all other contract
      fields. No FipContract value re-enters the Step 1
      fixpoint loop; the absorption table at lines 418-444 is
      applied at Step 5a once per function per SCC
      topological order (PL-1a).

      Convergence is therefore trivially guaranteed: the
      FipContract value space has 4 tiers (or 4 + u32 budgets
      for Bounded variants), and the Step 5a verification
      pass produces a single deterministic value per
      function. The N-ary join across observation sites is
      a single-pass fold (per L-1 + L-2 fold-order
      independence); the realization-time verification at
      fip.rs lines 75-200 is bounded by the IR size.

    Coverage gate — corpus enumeration:

      The shipped `FipContract::join` + `verify_fip_contract`
      are exercised by the interprocedural test surface in
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/interprocedural/
      tests.rs + the FIP verification test suite at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/verify/fip/
      tests.rs. Each row below pins one or more (P1)..(P6)
      conjuncts:

        Row F1: never_absorbs_certified —
                join_fip_contract([Never, Certified]) returns
                Never; (P1) Never-absorbs-all pin
        Row F2: never_absorbs_bounded —
                join_fip_contract([Never, Bounded(5)]) returns
                Never; (P1) Never-absorbs-all pin (Bounded
                variant)
        Row F3: never_absorbs_conditional —
                join_fip_contract([Never, Conditional])
                returns Never; (P1) Never-absorbs-all pin
                (Conditional variant)
        Row F4: never_join_never_idempotent —
                join_fip_contract([Never, Never]) returns
                Never; (P1) idempotence pin (L-3)
        Row F5: conditional_absorbs_bounded —
                join_fip_contract([Conditional, Bounded(7)])
                returns Conditional; (P2) Conditional-absorbs
                pin (Bounded variant)
        Row F6: conditional_absorbs_certified —
                join_fip_contract([Conditional, Certified])
                returns Conditional; (P2) Conditional-absorbs
                pin (Certified variant)
        Row F7: conditional_self_idempotent —
                join_fip_contract([Conditional, Conditional])
                returns Conditional; (P2) idempotence pin
                (L-3)
        Row F8: bounded_max_join_smaller_left —
                join_fip_contract([Bounded(3), Bounded(7)])
                returns Bounded(7); (P3) max-join pin
        Row F9: bounded_max_join_smaller_right —
                join_fip_contract([Bounded(7), Bounded(3)])
                returns Bounded(7); (P3) max-join pin
                (commutativity, L-1)
        Row F10: bounded_max_join_equal —
                 join_fip_contract([Bounded(5), Bounded(5)])
                 returns Bounded(5); (P3) idempotence pin
                 (L-3)
        Row F11: bounded_join_certified_preserves_budget —
                 join_fip_contract([Bounded(5), Certified])
                 returns Bounded(5); (P4) Bounded-wins pin
        Row F12: certified_join_bounded_preserves_budget —
                 join_fip_contract([Certified, Bounded(5)])
                 returns Bounded(5); (P4) Bounded-wins pin
                 (commutativity, L-1)
        Row F13: certified_self_idempotent —
                 join_fip_contract([Certified, Certified])
                 returns Certified; (P5) idempotence pin
                 (L-3)
        Row F14: certified_iff_zero_alloc_zero_dealloc —
                 function with zero `Construct` / `Reuse` /
                 `CollectionReuse` / `PartialApply` AND zero
                 unmatched `RcDec`-triggering-free events at
                 Step 5a observation → FipContract::Certified;
                 (P5) IFF pin (positive direction)
        Row F15: certified_downgraded_on_unmatched_alloc —
                 function provisionally seeded
                 FipContract::Certified at Step 1, but Step 5a
                 observes one unmatched `Construct` instruction
                 → FipContract::Bounded(1); (P5) IFF pin
                 (downgrade direction)
        Row F16: certified_downgraded_on_unmatched_dealloc —
                 function provisionally seeded Certified at
                 Step 1, but Step 5a observes one unmatched
                 `RcDec`-triggering-free → FipContract::
                 Conditional (caller-precondition); (P5) IFF
                 pin (dealloc-downgrade direction)
        Row F17: step_5a_runs_after_step_5 —
                 pipeline ordering test: Step 5a's
                 verify_fip_contract observes the post-Step-5
                 IR, NOT the pre-Step-5 IR; (P6) timing pin
        Row F18: scc_topological_callees_before_callers —
                 SCC fixpoint test: mutual-recursion SCC where
                 A calls B and vice versa; both A and B
                 complete Step 5a before any caller of the
                 SCC enters Step 5a; (P6) PL-1a topological
                 ordering pin
        Row F19: fipcontract_excluded_from_ic7_iteration —
                 IC-7 convergence bound test: the iteration
                 bound formula param_count × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4
                 does NOT include a FipContract height term;
                 FipContract values do not contribute to Step 1
                 fixpoint iteration count; (P6) IC-7 exclusion
                 pin
        Row F20: associativity_three_way_join —
                 join_fip_contract([Bounded(3), Bounded(7),
                 Conditional]) returns Conditional; verifies
                 L-2 associativity across all 4 tiers
        Row F21: never_associativity_three_way —
                 join_fip_contract([Certified, Bounded(5),
                 Never]) returns Never; verifies L-2
                 associativity with Never absorption
                 dominating

      Coverage axes:
        Absorption-table correctness (P1)..(P4):
          Rows F1-F12 directly pin each absorption rule across
          all 4-tier value combinations
        Idempotence (L-3):
          Rows F4, F7, F10, F13 pin self-join idempotence for
          each tier
        Commutativity (L-1):
          Rows F9, F12 pin commutativity (Bounded order
          irrelevance, Certified-Bounded order irrelevance)
        Associativity (L-2):
          Rows F20, F21 pin three-way associativity across
          mixed tiers
        Certification IFF (P5):
          Rows F14-F16 pin the bi-implication between Certified
          contract and zero-unmatched-alloc / zero-unmatched-
          dealloc in realized IR; positive direction (F14) +
          downgrade directions (F15, F16)
        Timing POST-REALIZATION (P6):
